WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.780
There are many ways that we use color.
Artists use different colors to express

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.650
emotions.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:14.259
Marketers attach color to their brands to
make them more recognizable.

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:17.730
Animals use colors to ward off predators

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.679
or attract mates-- and people do, too. What is color?

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:27.580
Scientists define color at the limited
range of light that we humans can see.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:32.320
We classify different types of light
based on their wavelength,

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.500
which can be anywhere from many meters in between each wave

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.860
peak to lengths as short as the diameter of an atom.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.920
The full electromagnetic spectrum

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.640
ranges from high-energy light waves 
with short wavelengths like cosmic and gamma rays

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.780
to low energy light waves with long
wavelengths

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.539
like radio waves. The light we are able to
see, called the

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:57.480
visible light spectrum, ranges from
violet light,

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.710
which has a wavelength of about  400 nanometers, to red light,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.820
which has a longer wavelength about 650
to 700 nanometers.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.119
But how do we perceive the visible
light spectrum?

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:12.650
When light bounces off an object, like this

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.439
painting, into our eyes, it first hits the
cornea,

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:19.840
a film that lays on top of our eyes. This layer

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.960
bends light towards the pupil, which
dilates or contracts to let in more or

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.590
less light.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.210
Once light passes through the pupil, it
is sent to the lens,

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.790
which angles and focuses the light to a point on the outer layer of the retina.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.799
Here, light-sensitive cells

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:43.180
called rods and cones send information
to ganglion nerve cells in the inner retina.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.579
Ganglion cells then send the information
to the optic nerve,

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:50.259
which sends the signal to the brain,
where the signal is processed and

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:51.250
interpreted.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.840
Rods and cones, the two types of
light sensitive cells

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.509
located on the outer retina, have
different jobs.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:03.700
Rods are responsible for our perception
of light and dark

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.300
and our peripheral vision. Cones are what
allow us to see color.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.610
They are found in the center of the retina,  where light is

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:15.640
focused. Cones come in three varieties,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.200
each sensitive to a different light. Interestingly,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.460
we have more cones sensitive to red light,
meaning our vision is best for warmer

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.170
colors like

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.550
reds, oranges and yellows.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.570
However, our cones aren't sensitive to light with wavelengths shorter

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.640
than 400 nanometers or longer
than around 700 nanometers.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.820
For example, a gamma wave's wavelength

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.910
is about the size of an atomic
nuclei,

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:48.390
which is much too short for any of our
three types of cones to sense

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.740
Radio waves are too long, with wavelengths

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.570
longer than two Empire State buildings put together. That doesn't mean that these

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.410
forms of light

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.720
aren't visible, per se. Other animals

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.740
have different kinds and number of cones, which allows them to see the world

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.130
differently than we do.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:11.650
Dogs, for example, are more limited in the wavelengths

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.750
they can see because they only have two
types of cones,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.380
leaving them "colorblind" to differences
between red and green.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.350
Butterflies have four types of cones in their eyes,

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.780
letting them see ultraviolet light.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.700
The animal with the best color vision
might be the mantis shrimp,

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.770
which has 12 different kinds of cones.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.930
If Pablo Picasso and Paul Klee

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.070
created amazing works of art with just
three types of cones,

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:45.620
imagine the masterpiece the mantis shrimp would create with 12!

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.850
good

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.590
home

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:59.420
for the where

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.570
them

